course: dictionary
lesson: 2548
title: Definitely: no doubt at all
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Definitely» means certainly, without any doubt. When you say «I will definitely be there», you are telling people you are sure. The words around it show this: the speaker is promising, not guessing.
explain: Use the swap check. Put «certainly» in place of «definitely»: «I will certainly be there» still makes sense, so the meanings are close. Now try «I might definitely be there». That sounds wrong, because «might» means not sure, and «definitely» means sure.
explain: Grandma said, «I definitely posted it.» Sam's brother said the parcel never came. Grandma's word shows she was certain that she had posted it, even though the parcel was lost.
fact: «Definitely» means certainly, without any doubt.
fact: «Definitely» can be used as a strong «yes»: «Are you coming?» «Definitely!»
fact: Words like «maybe» and «probably» show doubt, so they are the opposite of «definitely».
fact: Common phrases include «definitely not», «most definitely» and «I definitely think».
fact: «Deffo» is a short, friendly way of saying «definitely» (UK slang).
exercise: Grandma said, «I definitely posted it.» What does definitely show? | *She was completely sure | She was guessing and hoping | She was not sure at all | She was cross with the post office
exercise: Which word means the same as «definitely» in «Are you coming?» «Definitely!» | Never | *Certainly | Perhaps | Maybe
exercise: Which word has the opposite feeling of «definitely»? | Certainly | Surely | Truly | *Maybe
test: Sam asked, «Will you definitely come?» Mia said, «Definitely not.» What does Mia mean? | She is not sure yet | *No, certainly not | Yes, certainly | Maybe later
test: Which sentence shows the most certainty? | I probably come. | I could come, perhaps. | *I most definitely will come. | I might come.
exam: Choose the best word: The answer is ____ four, because two plus two is four. | perhaps | maybe | *definitely | sometimes
